INFORMATION 9 — CASE STUDY: ANTIBIOTIC RESISTANCE IN BACTERIA

Hospitals around the world face a serious crisis: certain bacteria such as Staphylococcus aureus have become resistant to antibiotics that were once extremely effective against them. Patients with infections caused by these 'superbugs' are very difficult to treat, and many die from infections that ordinary antibiotics could have cured 30 years ago.

When an antibiotic is used, the vast majority of bacteria in the patient's body are killed — but a small number of bacteria carry mutations that make them resistant to the drug. These resistant bacteria survive, multiply rapidly (bacteria can divide every 20 minutes), and pass their resistance genes to their offspring. Over many generations, the entire population becomes resistant.

Use the case study above and your knowledge of evolutionary biology to discuss the rise of antibiotic-resistant bacteria. In your essay you must:
• Describe the main points (mechanism) of Charles Darwin's theory of natural selection;
• Apply each of these points to the development of antibiotic resistance in the bacterial population;
• Explain why Lamarck's theory of the inheritance of acquired characteristics CANNOT satisfactorily explain antibiotic resistance;
• Suggest TWO practical actions that doctors and patients can take to slow the spread of antibiotic resistance.

Your response should be in the form of a coherent piece of writing using scientific terminology. Marks will be awarded for both content and synthesis.
